WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:02.899
Hi everyone. In this lesson,

2
00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:05.540
we're going to talk about Nanite and virtual shadow maps.

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.339
We're going to go over how those workflows allow you

4
00:00:08.339 --> 00:00:10.060
to be able to use them with Lumen

5
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.289
and to efficiently get things the way that you need them.

6
00:00:13.289 --> 00:00:15.369
We're going to break this up in a couple of videos.

7
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:17.859
So let's go ahead and get started.

8
00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:20.160
So most people ask why Nanite?

9
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.660
Well, it's Unreal 5's virtualized geometry system

10
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.179
stored as hierarchical system

11
00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:29.329
of smaller parts using triangles.

12
00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.129
And it adds a geometric complexity

13
00:00:31.129 --> 00:00:32.770
with less issues.

14
00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.429
So what that means is the older versions

15
00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:38.369
of using levels of detail in Unreal

16
00:00:38.369 --> 00:00:41.729
were done via a specific piece of geometry

17
00:00:41.729 --> 00:00:43.090
that was swapped out.

18
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:46.070
A lower medium and sometimes several mediums.

19
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.969
And then a high version when you're up close

20
00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:52.490
would be put into engine so that when your camera

21
00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:55.170
drew farther or near to the object,

22
00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.649
it would swap out the appropriate piece.

23
00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:01.289
That no longer is the case when using Nanite

24
00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:03.850
and using Lumen and virtual shadow maps together

25
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:04.750
as a whole.

26
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.730
Nanite now uses triangles.

27
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:11.409
So it does it more on a more micro base

28
00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:14.170
so that you can actually get the details still

29
00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:17.209
just by swapping out the triangle pieces

30
00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:18.670
for the particular section

31
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.450
where the camera's closest to it.

32
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.769
So you can really see that you're not swapping out

33
00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:25.689
a whole item, but you're swapping out little pieces

34
00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:28.930
almost like a puzzle giving you the highest fidelity

35
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.730
as quickly and as efficiently as possible.

36
00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.769
So Nanite allows for higher visual fidelity

37
00:01:35.769 --> 00:01:37.010
and works well with Lumen.

38
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.689
So you wanna pair that with Lumen

39
00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:40.930
as much as you can in virtual shadow maps.

40
00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.750
Outside of Lumen generating those shadow maps

41
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.909
like you're using the old school baking method

42
00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:49.810
will take too long on a complicated mesh.

43
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:50.849
It can be used.

44
00:01:50.849 --> 00:01:52.469
It really kind of depends on your scene,

45
00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:55.870
but it's just, you just gotta be careful with it.

46
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.989
So it's better because you don't have to bake

47
00:01:57.989 --> 00:02:00.109
to use it with Lumen in virtual shadow maps.

48
00:02:00.109 --> 00:02:03.030
So you get the updates on the fly, no baking involved.

49
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.510
And you can get that high visual fidelity

50
00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.150
for your models ready to go and on the fly

51
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.009
and get your updates as you go.

52
00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.590
And it's really fantastic.

53
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.789
So virtual shadow maps are used for more realistic results.

54
00:02:15.789 --> 00:02:17.430
We'll talk about in just a second.

55
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.250
So instance object count and draw calls

56
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.819
are less of a limitation now using Nanite.

57
00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:26.780
Now instances are in the millions.

58
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.740
So auto LODs and streaming uses clusters of data

59
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.180
instead of object based, like I mentioned earlier.

60
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.539
So instead of swapping out the model pieces,

61
00:02:35.539 --> 00:02:37.340
you're swapping out sections of the model

62
00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.180
based on positioning of the camera.

63
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.780
It's pretty mind boggling and pretty awesome

64
00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.099
the way it's able to do this

65
00:02:43.099 --> 00:02:45.539
and the way that we have got it garnered

66
00:02:45.539 --> 00:02:46.860
to be able to meet your needs.

67
00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.180
It's fantastic.

68
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.740
So more visually accurate than displacement

69
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:51.900
and less shadow artifacts

70
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.099
because you will get those with displacement

71
00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:56.379
if you're not careful.

72
00:02:56.379 --> 00:02:58.520
Let's talk about that a bit.

73
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.020
So displacement is inaccurate with extreme shadowing changes

74
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:06.280
and light changes with higher displacement values

75
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.159
due to going beyond the object's bounds.

76
00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:10.639
So anytime you're introducing displacement

77
00:03:10.639 --> 00:03:12.879
and introducing that into an object,

78
00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:15.719
you're going beyond the actual shape of the object

79
00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:17.460
and you're trying to fool the eye

80
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:20.659
to make it look like that it has more detail

81
00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:23.099
than it actually really does.

82
00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:25.060
So bounds can be increased in the object.

83
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.919
You can do that to prevent any visual distortion,

84
00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:30.419
but you still can get some inaccurate shadows.

85
00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:34.659
So you can use that now with a model editing tools

86
00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:37.020
inside the details of the model editor,

87
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.439
but you gotta be careful with it.

88
00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:39.979
So just tread with caution.

89
00:03:39.979 --> 00:03:42.340
It's better to keep it within the model itself.

90
00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:45.939
So Nanite is great for a higher resolution model shadowing,

91
00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:48.860
keeping accuracy, working with virtual shadow maps.

92
00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.939
As you see right here, we have two lighting scenarios.

93
00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:57.129
One is using the standard baked lighting

94
00:03:57.129 --> 00:04:01.129
and a piece of geometry that is not Nanite.

95
00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:02.969
And you can see we have the shadows here,

96
00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:04.310
we have the geometry,

97
00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.169
but you'll notice there isn't really

98
00:04:07.169 --> 00:04:09.810
some nice, smooth, soft shadows going on,

99
00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.689
some nice degrading of the shadow

100
00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:15.210
away from its object of contact.

101
00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.569
Over here, you can see it.

102
00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:18.970
You can see a nice, harder shadow here,

103
00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.649
and it starts to degrade realistically.

104
00:04:21.649 --> 00:04:24.930
And you can see we have a higher poly model

105
00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.050
converted to Nanite,

106
00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.689
and you can see every single shingle

107
00:04:28.689 --> 00:04:31.189
from the roof in here, and it's fantastic.

108
00:04:31.189 --> 00:04:33.569
So it really just bump things up.

109
00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:35.689
It can be used for ICV effects.

110
00:04:35.689 --> 00:04:37.329
This can be used for linear workflows,

111
00:04:37.329 --> 00:04:43.149
and again, also for games.

112
00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:45.930
So you might ask again still,

113
00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.810
what are some of the cool things overall

114
00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:49.810
with Nanite when it comes to visual,

115
00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:50.649
I mean, distortion?

116
00:04:50.649 --> 00:04:52.589
I mean, can you still get some issues?

117
00:04:52.589 --> 00:04:55.490
Like, can you still run into some problems with that?

118
00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:58.290
Like, you know, I mean, there could be some hard corners.

119
00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.750
Well, older versions of Nanite

120
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.149
used to rely on tangent space,

121
00:05:02.149 --> 00:05:05.129
but now Nanite supports explicit tangents

122
00:05:05.129 --> 00:05:06.769
in the data format and runtime.

123
00:05:06.769 --> 00:05:09.569
So you're going to get more accurate shapes

124
00:05:09.569 --> 00:05:10.410
within the model.

125
00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.889
So that's another benefit

126
00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:14.009
versus trying to do it via texture,

127
00:05:14.009 --> 00:05:15.829
trying to do it via displacement.

128
00:05:15.829 --> 00:05:18.529
We're doing it through the actual geometry,

129
00:05:18.529 --> 00:05:21.519
which is way better.

130
00:05:21.519 --> 00:05:24.680
So you can activate Nanite pretty easily in your system.

131
00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.120
Let's go and jump into Unreal here,

132
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.879
and I'll show you how to turn it on,

133
00:05:28.879 --> 00:05:32.220
and some things to keep in mind when working with it.

134
00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:35.300
So here I have a simple scene with some Nanite geometry.

135
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:36.500
The one that I'm accessing

136
00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.100
if you're following along for the class,

137
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.939
is you'll find that underneath,

138
00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:43.100
if we go into our class folder,

139
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.300
and in our class folder,

140
00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.939
you're going to see that there's a Nanite example

141
00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:48.420
called Lumen Nanite.

142
00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.660
Click on that and go ahead and click on the scene,

143
00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.199
and you should be right as rain.

144
00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:54.259
In the scene itself,

145
00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:56.860
you'll see that we have several pieces of geometry,

146
00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.779
and we also have this simple piece,

147
00:05:58.779 --> 00:06:00.620
this simple rock here on the bottom.

148
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.170
I'm going to click on him for a moment.

149
00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:07.240
So let's take a look at him in the content browser.

150
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:11.420
So to be able to turn anything into a Nanite object,

151
00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:12.980
you just right click it,

152
00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.439
and you want to go to Nanite,

153
00:06:14.439 --> 00:06:17.560
and you can turn on Nanite here.

154
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.800
Once Nanite is active,

155
00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.680
you can go and hit double click on the model itself,

156
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:23.920
and you can look at Nanite,

157
00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.480
and be able to adjust things according to what you need.

158
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.360
Now we get into these a bit deeper

159
00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:29.720
in our optimization course,

160
00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.279
so we're not going to get into it too much here,

161
00:06:32.279 --> 00:06:35.259
but you will notice there's enable Nanite support.

162
00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:37.879
This used to have to be turned on in the past,

163
00:06:37.879 --> 00:06:39.560
but now it automatically turns on

164
00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.620
when you activate the object to become a Nanite object.

165
00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.639
Down below you'll notice there's a max edge length factor.

166
00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.680
If you're using spline,

167
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.279
if you have like maybe a spline construction tool,

168
00:06:52.279 --> 00:06:56.639
and maybe you're making long pipes and wires and cables,

169
00:06:56.639 --> 00:06:59.139
and you have them attached to that blueprint,

170
00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:02.279
you may run into some visual issues that may occur

171
00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:04.519
if you don't adjust this number here.

172
00:07:04.519 --> 00:07:08.259
You can start off with 0.5 or even one.

173
00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:09.639
If you run into issues where the,

174
00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:11.779
as the camera moves around,

175
00:07:11.779 --> 00:07:15.660
the level of detail shifts and becomes a very low poly.

176
00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:16.839
If that happens,

177
00:07:16.839 --> 00:07:21.920
just go ahead and go into your model details,

178
00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:22.939
and under Nanite,

179
00:07:22.939 --> 00:07:25.560
go ahead and go to your max edge length factor

180
00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.089
and adjust this number if that occurs.

181
00:07:30.089 --> 00:07:34.970
Also something to consider when working with Nanite,

182
00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:36.529
is you want to make sure

183
00:07:36.529 --> 00:07:39.889
that you're not combining large pieces of geometry

184
00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:43.939
with each other inside of Unreal.

185
00:07:43.939 --> 00:07:45.879
We'll talk about that a bit later

186
00:07:45.879 --> 00:07:47.560
in our optimization course,

187
00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.000
but what I mean by that,

188
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.319
if this is a Nanite object,

189
00:07:51.319 --> 00:07:54.079
and this is intended also to be a Nanite object,

190
00:07:54.079 --> 00:07:56.509
and you decided to merge them,

191
00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:59.910
and even you can even convert that merge after the fact,

192
00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.209
you kind of want to avoid it

193
00:08:01.209 --> 00:08:04.149
because there becomes an issue of calculation

194
00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:08.149
that isn't quite as efficient as we'd like for it to be.

195
00:08:08.149 --> 00:08:10.089
So you want to keep them separate,

196
00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:13.069
and Unreal's fine with that and works just great.

197
00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:15.709
It allows it to isolate that piece of geometry,

198
00:08:15.709 --> 00:08:18.829
apply the Nanite system to that object,

199
00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:20.350
and keeping them individual.

200
00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:25.560
That also helps you if you're going to modulate things.

201
00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:26.839
One thing I want to mention

202
00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:30.279
is you want to turn on virtual textures when you can.

203
00:08:30.279 --> 00:08:32.559
They're not required to be used with Nanite,

204
00:08:32.559 --> 00:08:34.200
but they are highly recommended,

205
00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.980
specifically for games with large worlds.

206
00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.080
That allows you to manage that overhead

207
00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.850
so that things are going in the right direction.

208
00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:46.210
So there's also a fallback tangent setting

209
00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:50.620
in the Nanite static mesh editor.

210
00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:51.940
This is a menu option

211
00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.179
that provides more explicit control

212
00:08:54.179 --> 00:08:58.419
over which tangent to reduce to for the fallback mesh,

213
00:08:58.419 --> 00:09:01.860
relative error or percent triangles.

214
00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:05.279
It helps choose which heuristic to use

215
00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:07.879
when generating a static mesh.

216
00:09:07.879 --> 00:09:09.340
Auto should work fine,

217
00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:12.399
but the others are in play for more finite control

218
00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:14.259
if the mesh is overly complicated

219
00:09:14.259 --> 00:09:17.500
or has display issues and et cetera.

220
00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:18.860
We dive deeper in these things

221
00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:23.990
when we start tackling optimization.

222
00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.389
To quickly check which objects you have

223
00:09:26.389 --> 00:09:28.009
that are Nanite and not,

224
00:09:28.009 --> 00:09:30.970
you can simply run this particular text here

